WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.821
Every IVF index in FAISS starts as twenty-five full passes of k-means.

00:00:04.821 --> 00:00:07.341
What if two or three were enough?

00:00:10.793 --> 00:00:11.737
Hi, I'm Wrenly!

00:00:11.737 --> 00:00:14.567
This is e-means, from Meta FAIR, now in FAISS.

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:17.084
Let's see why it needs so few passes.

00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:19.840
Chapter one.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.396
The cost of being exact.

00:00:26.175 --> 00:00:28.577
Lloyd's algorithm alternates two exact moves.

00:00:28.577 --> 00:00:31.379
Assign every point to its nearest centroid.

00:00:31.799 --> 00:00:34.032
Then move each centroid to the mean of its points.

00:00:34.999 --> 00:00:37.202
One update per full pass.

00:00:37.202 --> 00:00:41.608
The FAISS default is twenty-five passes, so twenty-five centroid moves.

00:00:42.028 --> 00:00:45.800
And since the loss only goes down, a bad start never gets fixed.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:46.670
It gets polished.

00:00:48.508 --> 00:00:49.968
The authors' toy shows it.

00:00:49.968 --> 00:00:52.888
Each centroid jumps all the way to its cluster's mean.

00:00:53.308 --> 00:00:58.177
By iteration twenty-five, two centroids share a blob, and one is stranded between two.

00:00:59.675 --> 00:01:00.258
Chapter two.

00:01:00.258 --> 00:01:01.716
Forget at a fixed rate.

00:01:07.192 --> 00:01:10.618
Mini-batch k-means adds every batch to a running sum forever.

00:01:10.618 --> 00:01:13.358
Its step shrinks fast, and it never forgets.

00:01:13.778 --> 00:01:15.401
So it stalls above Lloyd.

00:01:15.401 --> 00:01:18.322
e-means fades old statistics at a fixed rate instead.

00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:20.608
Here's the idea.

00:01:20.608 --> 00:01:24.309
A moving average of each cluster's count and sum, many small steps

00:01:24.309 --> 00:01:27.085
per pass, and fitted laws for batch and step.

00:01:29.525 --> 00:01:30.649
Here is the formula.

00:01:31.525 --> 00:01:35.009
Rewritten, the update is a move toward the cluster's mean in this batch.

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:38.580
How far depends on the step, and on this batch's count

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:39.727
over the faded count.

00:01:39.727 --> 00:01:42.593
A cluster that suddenly gets many points moves a lot.

00:01:43.013 --> 00:01:46.118
The weight is between zero and one, so it never overshoots.

00:01:46.538 --> 00:01:49.874
Make the batch the whole dataset, with a step of one, and

00:01:49.874 --> 00:01:51.264
this is one Lloyd iteration.

00:01:51.264 --> 00:01:53.210
Lloyd is a corner of this family.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.867
One update: draw a mini-batch, assign it, sum per cluster.

00:01:58.287 --> 00:02:01.592
Fold the sums into the moving averages, and the centroids follow.

00:02:02.012 --> 00:02:05.030
The step anneals on a cosine, down to a tenth of its peak.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.679
Every five updates, a starving cluster is rebuilt by splitting a busy donor.

00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:12.871
In the last epoch, each centroid is set to the exact

00:02:12.871 --> 00:02:13.878
mean of its points.

00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:17.770
Let's race them on a toy.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.576
Five blobs, and one bad start: two centroids in one blob, one

00:02:21.576 --> 00:02:22.845
stranded between two others.

00:02:23.265 --> 00:02:27.587
Lloyd moves every centroid all the way to its cluster's mean, every pass.

00:02:27.587 --> 00:02:29.582
It converges fast, and stays stuck.

00:02:30.002 --> 00:02:31.313
e-means draws small batches.

00:02:31.313 --> 00:02:34.917
Each centroid steps part of the way toward its batch mean.

00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:39.504
The noise pulls the crowded pair apart, and a centroid finds the empty blob.

00:02:39.924 --> 00:02:41.190
Same points, same start.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.671
Many small noisy steps found what one exact step could not.

00:02:46.175 --> 00:02:47.277
The split matters.

00:02:47.277 --> 00:02:51.318
With no revival, four thousand three hundred and thirteen clusters die.

00:02:51.318 --> 00:02:52.787
With the split, forty-seven.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:55.551
Chapter three.

00:02:55.551 --> 00:02:56.678
No sweep required.

00:03:01.758 --> 00:03:04.936
A method that needs a sweep has lost to Lloyd already.

00:03:04.936 --> 00:03:08.403
Left: for each batch size, the best step, the stars, moves right

00:03:08.403 --> 00:03:09.558
as the batch grows.

00:03:09.978 --> 00:03:12.986
Right: loss is flat across batch sizes, until a knee.

00:03:13.406 --> 00:03:17.111
Both are fitted as power laws, on about seventy-five thousand runs.

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:23.150
Take the paper's ablation setting: a million points, three epochs.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:27.095
The batch law gives sixty-five thousand five hundred and thirty-six.

00:03:27.515 --> 00:03:29.684
The step law, zero point three six one.

00:03:30.455 --> 00:03:31.810
That is forty-eight updates.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:32.826
Lloyd makes three.

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:37.098
About four points per cluster per update, against Lloyd's sixty-one.

00:03:37.098 --> 00:03:40.565
Sixteen noisy moves per pass, against one exact one.

00:03:42.217 --> 00:03:42.766
Chapter four.

00:03:42.766 --> 00:03:43.863
Does it hold up?

00:03:49.217 --> 00:03:52.201
Loss against epochs, where one is Lloyd at twenty-five.

00:03:52.201 --> 00:03:55.516
Blue, e-means, drops below the line within a few epochs.

00:03:55.936 --> 00:03:58.337
The orange mini-batch baselines flatten above it.

00:03:58.337 --> 00:04:03.139
Across the main table, e-means needs one point four to two point six epochs.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.915
In GPU wall-clock time, the paper reports nine point five times faster at worst.

00:04:10.335 --> 00:04:13.373
And up to forty-five point five times at a billion points.

00:04:13.793 --> 00:04:17.311
Above ten million there is an extra factor the epochs don't explain,

00:04:17.311 --> 00:04:19.363
and the paper doesn't break it down.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:22.906
Three things are smaller than the headline.

00:04:23.739 --> 00:04:26.036
The loss gain is fractions of a percent.

00:04:26.036 --> 00:04:27.184
The story is compute.

00:04:27.604 --> 00:04:31.397
Against well-seeded Lloyd, it wins every case only at five epochs.

00:04:31.817 --> 00:04:34.350
And the laws were fitted on high-dimensional embeddings.

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:38.585
Lloyd was the costliest corner of a bigger family.

00:04:38.585 --> 00:04:42.654
Many small, noisy, scheduled steps get there in a tenth of the passes.

00:04:44.858 --> 00:04:45.512
To recap.

00:04:45.512 --> 00:04:50.087
A moving average, a cosine step, a split for the starving, and fitted laws.

00:04:50.087 --> 00:04:51.068
Thanks for watching!

00:04:51.488 --> 00:04:53.589
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:53.589 --> 00:04:54.189
I'm Wrenly.

00:04:54.189 --> 00:04:54.489
Bye!
